  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Daily precipitation chances tonight and \ntomorrow with a strong cold front yielding a chance at severe \nweather on Tuesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nNUCAPS soundings from 1540Z and 1724Z showed that earlier\ninversion seen above 500 mb has eroded, but CAPE remains skinny\nwith MUCAPE in the 300-500 J/kg range from roughly Hagerstown\nsoutheast to Fredericksburg. A shortwave-trough dropping into\nthe area in NW flow later this evening is expected to aid in\nconvective development with HREF ensembles showing the best\nchance of convection between 23Z and 03Z. Given the skinny/weak\nCAPE seen on the NUCAPS data, severe thunderstorms appear\nunlikely this evening. \n\nWarmer and slightly more unstable conditions ared expected Monday,\nbut shortwave ridging aloft may limit thunderstorm coverage. \nOverall, it looks like less coverage Monday afternoon than \ntoday.\n\nA deepening shortwave-trough will drop from the eastern Great\nLks into our area Tue afternoon pushing a strong cold front \nthrough the area Tue evening. Strong height falls coupled with \nstrengthening winds aloft will lead to greater thunderstorm \ncoverage and storm organization with a much greater risk of \nsevere thunderstorms. Damaging wind gusts appear to be primary \nrisk due to expected fast storm motions, but isolated tornadoes \ncan't never be ruled out in NW flow. Convection should start \nexiting the area quickly after 00Z Wed. One caveat is potential\nfor showers and thunderstorms early Tue morning which may leave\nbehind plenty of clouds and limit instability through the day\nTue. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Drier conditions return mid to late week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nIn the wake of Tuesday's frontal boundary, the associated low will \nslowly meander towards the northeast over the next couple of days. \nThis should reduce rain chances overall, but spotty showers and \nthunderstorms remain possible as this low slowly slides offshore of \nNew England late in the week. High pressure moves in overhead \ntowards the end of the week bringing drier air and similar \nconditions overall as the week prior, with highs in the low to mid \n80s.\n\nHeading into next weekend, a broad area of low pressure begins to \nform over the Midwest and track eastward. As temperatures gradually \nrise towards more normal July averages, this could bring additional \nshower and thunderstorm chances to the region.",
